{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And Israel settled in the land of Goshen, etc.</b> This whole verse speaks in accusation against the children of Israel, for the Holy One, blessed be He, decreed upon them, ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״ (Genesis 15:13), yet they sought to be permanent settlers in the place where ger-status was decreed upon them — as they expounded concerning Jacob, ״וַיֵּשֶׁב יַעֲקֹב״ (Genesis 37:1): he sought to settle in tranquility, so the rage of Joseph sprang upon him, etc. The verse accuses them for this settling, that they sought a holding in a land not theirs — and this is not what they said to Pharaoh: ״לָגוּר בָּאָרֶץ בָּאנוּ״ (Genesis 47:4), which teaches that at first they did not go down to take root there but to sojourn, like the resident of a lodging, and now they went back on their word. And they became so rooted there that they did not want to leave Egypt, until the Holy One, blessed be He, had to bring them out from there with a strong hand, and those who did not want to leave died in the three days of darkness." "And it says, <b>״וַיִּפְרוּ וַיִּרְבּוּ מְאֹד״,</b> and adjacent to it is ״וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב״, hinting in this juxtaposition to what our Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said (Ta'anit 5a): ״יַעֲקֹב לֹא מֵת״ — Jacob did not die. And the Gemara asks: was it for nothing that the embalmers embalmed him, etc.? And it answers: I am expounding a verse: ״אַל תִּירָא יַעֲקֹב״ וְגוֹ׳ ״וְשָׁב זַרְעוֹ מֵאֶרֶץ שִׁבְיָם״ (Jeremiah 31:16) — Scripture juxtaposes him to his seed: just as his seed is alive, so too he is alive. And one may ask here that He gave him no answer at all to the question 'was it for nothing that the embalmers embalmed him,' etc. And the explanation of the matter is thus: everyone who leaves behind children is considered as though he did not die, for the child is an offshoot of his mother's womb and the shoot of his father, and a part of the father is included in the son. When the son is alive, then the part of his father included in him is also alive. And specifically Jacob, whose bed was complete and all of them were righteous, as is written (see Rashi, Genesis 18:19): 'whoever raises a righteous son is as though he did not die' — specifically a righteous son; but a wicked son is considered in his lifetime as dead, ״כָּשַׁל עוֹזֵר וְנָפַל עָזוּר״ (Isaiah 31:3), and there is no creature like this who merits the father. Therefore they said specifically ״יַעֲקֹב לֹא מֵת״ — even though the embalmers embalmed him, nevertheless when his seed is alive then he too, the part of him that is in them, is alive. And this matter is hinted at in this juxtaposition, for through ״וַיִּפְרוּ וַיִּרְבּוּ מְאֹד״ — and his seed was alive — therefore ״וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב״, that he too is alive."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:03.514879830Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}